Back to the original question, or rather the subsection on which language t=
o learn.=A0 If you are short on time or patience, then go for toki pona.=A0=
 Although the claim that you can learn it in a day is a bit optimistic, you=
 can produce reasonable sentences which say what you mean by the end of the=
 day and pretty much read everything by the end of a week of writing to the=
 various tp lists.=A0 The vocabulary is strange but, at only 125 words or s=
o long, easily mastered.=A0 The grammar is very restricted and simple; the =
hardest thing will be expressing any complex ideas (and dealing with number=
s, of which there are virtually none).=A0 There are a couple of unofficial =
lesson sets which are adequate for this language.=20
If you are a bit more ambitious, try Esperanto.=A0 You will probably be abl=
e to get the gist of things in Esperanto right from the start, since much o=
f the vocabulary is more or less familiar (the more so the more you know of=
 Latin,=A0 Greek, some Romance Languages, German, and English) and the gram=
mar is familiar from all the mentioned languages.=A0 The 16 Rules will get =
you through many of the details and learning the pronoun system takes care =
of much of the rest.=A0 But writing it is harder, since you don't know whic=
h of the available forms -- from all the languages listed -- are actually u=
sed for the meaning you have in mind.=A0 Sometimes your favorite is not use=
d at all and sometimes it is used but in a slightly different meaning from =
what you want.=A0 But international and scientific words are all available =
immediately.=A0 For English speakers, remembering agreement, plurals in j r=
ather than s, and the accusative case are the big problems.=A0 There are
 dozens of good primers in English (and every other major language).
Lojban is more ambitious still,=A0 since there are no familiar vocabulary a=
nd even the "basic" vocabulary is 3000 words or so.=A0 The grammar of simpl=
e sentences is simple but feels unfamiliar, since many "objects" are things=
 which would take prepositions in English.=A0 But once over that oddity, th=
ings run fairly smoothly for quite a while.=A0 In fact, almost none of the =
really complex pieces of Lojban are actually needed and those that are are =
easily analogized to familiar patterns in English.=A0 Unfortunately, there =
has not been until now a decent primer -- or even a really bad one, so the =
process of learning what you really need and what is window dressing or adv=
anced work has to be by trial and error.=A0 Using the beginners list is a g=
ood idea in that people on it tend to be at a lower level and still learnin=
g rudiments -- for all they do try to fly too early.=A0 Folks on this list =
tend to be a bit better and also terribly fond of esoterica -- not good
 role models, in short.




